ile fully read earlier. Add:\n\n```python\n    DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar.int_or_none_min(minimum=32)\n```\n\nUnder \"-- Runtime --\"? Maybe add a \"-- Logging --\" section. But instruction says preserve existing items; adding a section is fine. Careful: adding a new section is a modification; fine.\n\nNote the error message must contain the variable name — the descriptor wraps errors with f\"{self.name}: {e}\". Good.\n\nEmpty string: int(\"\") raises ValueError, with name prefix. Good.\n\nNow logger.py formatter. Need budget logic:\n\n```python\n_OMITTED_MARKER = \"...<omitted>...\"\n\nclass NewLineFormatter(logging.Formatter):\n    def __init__(self, fmt, datefmt=None, high_precision=False, use_color=False, *, max_message_chars=None):\n        super().__init__(fmt, datefmt)\n        if max_message_chars is not None:\n            if isinstance(max_message_chars, bool) or not isinstance(max_message_chars, int) or max_message_chars < 32:\n                raise ValueError(...)\n        self.max_message_chars = max_message_chars\n```\n\nformat: compute message content budget. Approach: compute record.getMessage() after replacing args, then truncate. But we need not modify record.msg/args. Common approach: build a copy of the record? The record's `message` attribute is set by super().format via record.getMessage(); also exc_text.\n\nSimplest: do parameter substitution ourselves:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    max_chars = self.max_message_chars\n    original_msg = record.msg\n    original_message = getattr(record, \"message\", None)  # careful\n```\n\nBetter approach: compute message text = record.getMessage() (which does msg % args). If it exceeds budget and keep_full_message is not True, temporarily set record.msg to truncated and record.args = None, call super().format(record), then restore record.msg/args. But super().format mutates record.message and record.exc_text (cached). Also record.exc_text caching could be fine.\n\nRequirement: \"不能改写共享原记录的 msg、args、exc_info、stack_info\" — restore them afterwards. And \"同一记录随后交给完整处理器，仍须保留完整消息\" — restoring ensures.\n\nBut also after we restore, record.message stays as truncated — the handler for \"complete\" processor calling super().format would call record.getMessage() which recomputes from msg/args (logging's Formatter.format does record.message = record.getMessage() each call). Yes, logging.Formatter.format always sets record.message = record.getMessage(). So fine.\n\nCareful: record.exc_text caching — Formatter.format caches exc_text if not already set; that's fine, unchanged.\n\nSo:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    max_chars = self.max_message_chars\n    saved_msg, saved_args = record.msg, record.args\n    try:\n        if max_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n            message = record.getMessage()\n            if len(message) > max_chars:\n                truncated = _truncate(message, max_chars)\n                record.msg = truncated\n                record.args = None\n        return self._format(record)\n    finally:\n        record.msg = saved_msg\n        record.args = saved_args\n```\n\nWait — but record.message set by super().format would then be the truncated message, and multi-line prefix logic uses record.message; consistent. But subsequent full handlers recompute. However if some other formatter uses record.message without recompute — logging.Formatter.format always recomputes so fine.\n\nAlso `msg = super().format(record)` and multi-line prefix; keep same. Let me restructure: rename existing body into `_format` or inline.\n\nActually I can keep the existing format body and wrap: define `format` calling `self._format_record(record)`.\n\nTruncation: marker len 15. remaining = max_chars - len(marker). head = ceil(remaining/2), tail = remaining - head. For 32: remaining=17, head=9, tail=8. ✓\n\n_getMessage with args: for `%s` etc. record.getMessage() handles dict args too.\n\nEdge: record.args may be a dict; setting to None fine since msg is fully substituted. But wait — when args is a dict and msg contains no format spec? getMessage handles. Fine.\n\nAnother subtlety: record.exc_info present — super().format appends traceback which shouldn't be counted. Our message computed excludes it. Good. stack_info ends up appended to record.message? In logging.Formatter.format: `if record.stack_info: s = s + self.formatStack(record.stack_info)` — appended to s after message; record.message only message. Good.\n\nBut careful: `msg.find(record.message)` for prefix — record.message is the truncated message, and msg (formatted output) contains it. Fine.\n\nHmm, one issue: if truncation happens, record.message = truncated; the multi-line prefix computation uses truncated. ok.\n\nAlso colors unchanged.\n\nNow _setup_logger: pass max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS. Need import in logger.py: `from deepinfer.environ import envs`. Check for circular imports: does environ import logger? environ imports os, threading, collections.abc, datetime, typing — no logger. Safe.\n\nBut careful: environ may import deepinfer something? Let's check with search. I'll do a quick search of \"import\" in environ.py — from the read lines 1-140, imports are stdlib only. Lines 140+ might have more. Let's just check quickly via search_code for \"from deepinfer\" in environ.py.\n\nAlso note initial environment may not have var set → None. If set to invalid at import time, _setup_logger at import would raise? _setup_logger called at module import; accessing config raises ValueError which is acceptable? Tests probably import logger module after setting env... Hmm, if a test sets env to invalid then imports logger, the module-level _setup_logger would raise at import. That would fail tests badly. Should we guard? The spec says default init reads config. If config invalid, ValueError is expected on access. Probably tests set env after import to test invalid values. But 